[38;2;124;124;124m\int_0^\infty (\partial_r \psi_n)^2 r \, dr \to 0$ as $n \to \infty$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThe functions $f_n$ should be chosen so that $\psi_n \in C^\infty((0,\infty); [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m[0,1])$ and are supported away from zero and identically one near $[1, \infty]$.[0m
[38;2;124;124;124mNote: $\psi$ is supported away from zero (i.e., $\psi(r) = 0$ for $r$ near $0$) [0m[38;2;124;124;124mand $\psi \equiv 1$ near $[1, \infty)$ (i.e., $\psi(r) = 1$ for $r \geq$ some [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mthreshold $\leq 1$).[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mSo $\psi$ is a smooth cutoff function that transitions from $0$ (near $0$) to [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m$1$ (near $1$ and beyond).[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mNow $\psi_n = \psi \circ f_n$. For $\psi_n$ to be supported away from zero and [0m
[38;2;124;124;124midentically one near $[1, \infty)$, we need:[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m- $\psi_n(r) = 0$ for $r$ near $0$: this means $\psi(f_n(r)) = 0$ for $r$ near [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m$0$, i.e., $f_n(r)$ is in the region where $\psi = 0$ (near $0$) for $r$ near [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m$0$.[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m- $\psi_n(r) = 1$ for $r$ near $[1, \infty)$: this means $\psi(f_n(r)) = 1$ for [0m[38;2;124;124;124m$r \geq$ some threshold, i.e., $f_n(r)$ is in the region where $\psi = 1$ (near [0m[38;2;124;124;124m$[1, \infty)$) for $r$ near $[1, \infty)$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThe simple scaling $\psi_n = \psi(2^n r)$ doesn't work because:[0m
