[38;2;124;124;124mSince $\psi'$ is smooth and supported on a compact subset of $(0, \infty)$ [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m(because $\psi$ is constant near $0$ and near $[1,\infty)$), the integral $[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m\int_0^\infty (\psi'(s))^2 s \, ds$ is a finite positive constant $C$. Therefore[0m[38;2;124;124;124m$I_n = C/n \to 0$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThe proof is complete. Let me write it up cleanly.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mActually, I should also note: the problem says $\psi$ is "identically one near [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m$[1, \infty]$". I interpret this as $\psi \equiv 1$ on $[1, \infty)$ (or on a [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mneighborhood of $[1, \infty)$, meaning $\psi(s) = 1$ for $s \geq s_0$ for some [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m$s_0 \leq 1$). And "supported away from zero" means $\psi(s) = 0$ for $s \leq [0m
[38;2;124;124;124ms_1$ for some $s_1 > 0$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mWith $f_n(r) = r^{1/n}$:[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m- For $r \geq 1$: $f_n(r) = r^{1/n} \geq 1 \geq s_0$, so $\psi_n(r) = 1$. ✓[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m- For $r \leq s_1^n$: $f_n(r) = r^{1/n} \leq s_1$, so $\psi_n(r) = 0$. So $[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m\psi_n$ is supported on $[s_1^n, \infty)$, which is away from $0$. ✓[0m

